```java
class Config {
    private static Config instance;
    static Config getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Config(); // not atomic — two threads can both pass the null check
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Under concurrent first access, two threads can both see `instance == null`, both construct a `Config`, and one of them silently discards its work — or worse, callers on different threads end up holding two different "singleton" instances.

**Double-checked locking** (needs `volatile`):

```java
class Config {
    private static volatile Config instance;
    static Config getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (Config.class) {
                if (instance == null) instance = new Config();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

**The simpler eager-holder alternative**, using the JVM's own class-loading guarantee instead of manual [locking](synchronization-techniques-in-oo-design):

```java
class Config {
    private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }
    static Config getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}
```

The holder class is only loaded (and `INSTANCE` constructed) on first access to `getInstance()`, and the JVM guarantees class initialization itself is thread-safe — no explicit lock needed.

```java
enum Config {
    INSTANCE;
    void doSomething() { /* ... */ }
}
```

Java guarantees enum constants are constructed exactly once, thread-safely, by the JVM itself — this sidesteps the whole race condition category rather than fixing it with locks. It's also serialization-safe by default, which the other two approaches need extra code to get right. The tradeoff: it reads unusually to developers unfamiliar with the idiom, so naming *why* it works is worth doing out loud.
